<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      Path reachability probe, every 60 seconds: explicit HTTP proxy on :1082 versus the transparent
      fake-IP path, for both venue hosts.  Read-only and unauthenticated (/fapi/v1/ping, weight 1);
      it never writes to the venue or to any live state directory.  Evidence for the NO_PROXY
      question raised by the transport failures of 2026-09-15; see deploy/run_proxy_probe.sh for
      what the two arms do and do NOT mean.
      Cadence: 60s, and this time the number is measured rather than estimated.  The 29 outages in the
      first 21h have a median duration of 138s (range 0-579s), so a 300s period would sample the
      median event with probability ~0.46 - fine for counting outages, useless for the one question
      still open, which needs the CONTROL arm to land INSIDE a cluster rather than near one.  At 60s
      every cluster longer than a minute is sampled on all three arms.  Cost is 4 requests per minute
      at weight 1 against a 2400/min budget; the two venue arms carry it, captive.apple.com is free.
      (It ran at 300s between 4492458b and its removal; that was a mistake for this purpose.)
      WHAT IS STILL OPEN, and why this was reinstalled.  The first question - "does the transparent
      path survive what the explicit one does not", i.e. is NO_PROXY a fix - is ANSWERED: 28 of 29
      clusters hit BOTH venue arms, so NO_PROXY is not a fix.  The control arm's question is NOT.
      It went live 2026-09-16T11:54:00Z and was torn down at 14:43Z, 2.83h later: 150 samples, zero
      failures, but only 2 clusters fell in that window and BOTH were single-host (fapi only), which
      the rule in run_proxy_probe.sh explicitly excludes from this reading.  Usable clusters: ZERO.
      "Control arm clean" therefore meant "never covered", NOT "remote node".  Do not read it as a
      verdict again.
      REMOVE ONLY AFTER a "both hosts" cluster has landed inside the control window.  Those run about
      one per 1.65h (13 in 21.45h); 2.83h returned 0 against an expectation of 1.7, so leave it up
      5-6 hours, not the expected value.  Read with: split clusters by host, keep only the ones after
      the control arm went live.
      Temporary by intent: a few days of samples, then read and remove.
      Install (operator action):
        cp deploy/com.beidou.proxy-probe.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
        launchctl load -w ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.beidou.proxy-probe.plist
      Remove (operator action):
        launchctl unload -w ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.beidou.proxy-probe.plist
        rm ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.beidou.proxy-probe.plist
    -->
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.beidou.proxy-probe</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>/Users/maguannan/beidou/deploy/run_proxy_probe.sh</string>
    </array>
    <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
    <string>/Users/maguannan/beidou</string>
    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <integer>60</integer>
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>
    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/Users/maguannan/Library/Application Support/beidou/proxy-probe.stdout.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/Users/maguannan/Library/Application Support/beidou/proxy-probe.stderr.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
